"Towards Poisoning Robustness Certification for Natural Language Generation (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.09757v1)\nciteturn31703view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn31698view0\",\"lineno\":219}); Total lines: 489\nL202: Agents and LLMs often operate in multi-turn settings. Consider a user who received example response (D) suggesting a potential harmful combination of chemicals. If the user replies asking “Are you sure?” the poisoning adversary is only successful if they maintain the poisoned output for both (or in the $n$-turn case all) responses. This dilutes their budget across inputs, enabling stronger collective certificates which as been shown in the case of DPA/hash bagging (cite50†Chen et al., 2022 ).\nL203: Following cite50†Chen et al. (2022) , we formulate collective certification as an optimization problem. For stability, we adapt their binary-integer program to NLG (see Appendix cite23†A ). For validity, their approach needs substantial non-trivial modifications; we introduce a correct multi-turn/collective certification proceedure with the following MILP formulation:\nL204: ###### Definition 4.7 (Collective TPA certificate).\nL205: \nL206: Let $\\{x^{(i)}\\}_{i=1}^{N}$ be $N$ test prompts, $K$ the global poisoning budget, $\\{\\mathcal{P}_{j}\\}_{j=1}^{M}$ the shards with base models $f_{j}$, and $s^{*}$, $\\Phi$, $t$, $v_{t}$ as in Algorithm cite71†1 . For each $x^{(i)}$, let $C_{s^{*}}^{(i)}=\\{c_{1}^{(i)},\\dots,c_{s^{*}}^{(i)}\\}$ be the top $s^{*}$ classes with votes $\\{v_{1}^{(i)},\\dots,v_{s^{*}}^{(i)}\\}$. The MILP is:\nL207:  |  | $\\displaystyle\\max_{a}\\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\mathbb{I}\\bigg[\\tau_{i}\\leq\\sum_{j=1}^{M}\\psi_{j}^{(i)}\\bigg]\\quad\\text{s.t.}$  |\nL208:  | $\\displaystyle\\psi_{j}^{(i)}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\mathbb{I}\\{a_{j}\\geq R_{j}(x^{(i)})\\}\\left(1+\\mathbb{I}\\{f_{j}(x^{(i)})\\in C_{s^{*}}^{(i)}\\}\\right),$  |\nL209:  | $\\displaystyle\\tau_{i}$  | $\\displaystyle=(\\mu-v_{t^{(i)}})+\\sum_{b=1}^{s^{*}}(v_{b}-\\mu),\\quad\\sum_{j=1}^{M}a_{j}=K,$  |\nL210:  | $\\displaystyle\\mu$  | $\\displaystyle=\\Phi[s^{*}\\!-\\!1]+\\frac{(V_{c}[s^{*}]-\\Phi[s^{*}\\!-\\!1]+1)\\cdot s^{*}}{s^{*}+1},$  |\nL211:  | $\\displaystyle R_{j}(x^{(i)})$  | $\\displaystyle=\\begin{cases}K+1&\\text{if }f_{j}(x^{(i)})\\neq t^{(i)}\\\\\nL212: 1&\\text{otherwise}\\end{cases},\\forall j.$  |\nL213: ###### Theorem 4.8.\nL214: \nL215: Let $\\mathcal{A}(K)$ denote the solution to the MILP in Definition cite73†4.7 . For any attack within budget $K$, at least $\\lfloor N\\cdot\\mathcal{A}(K)\\rfloor$ prompts maintain their original (safe) predictions.\nL216: \nL217: This collective certificate is strictly stronger than individual certificates when the adversary’s budget is insufficient to poison all prompts simultaneously, as we demonstrate empirically in Section cite19†5.3 .\nL218: cite74†Image: Refer to caption Figure 2: TPA certification results. (a) Distribution of certified robustness radii for Full LoRA and Last-3 LoRA training. Dashed lines indicate medians. (b) Accuracy comparison showing Full LoRA achieves 67% vs. 54% for Last-3 LoRA and 40% for zero-shot. (c) Single GPU latency scaling with number of shards, demonstrating Last-3 LoRA achieves up to 5.9$\\times$ speedup over Full LoRA; zero-shot inference takes 0.3s.\nL219: ## 5 Experimental Results\nL220: We empirically validate our framework across three settings. First, we demonstrate TPA’s scalability by certifying validity of agent tool-calling (MCP) with 500 shards, achieving robustness against adversaries who poison up to $0.5\\%$ of training data (§cite18†5.2 ). Second, we certify preference-based alignment showing that ensemble defenses maintain $>90\\%$ validity certification at $k=9$ poisoning budget with $20+$ token horizons (§cite19†5.3 ).\nL221: Third, we evaluate empirical robustness against practical backdoor attacks from cite55†Fu et al. (2024) , demonstrating that ensemble defenses reduce attack success rates from $40-48\\%$ to below $6\\%$ (§cite20†5.4 ).\nL222: ### 5.1 Evaluation Metrics\nL223: \nL224: Certified Response Radius. The simplest metric we study is the certified response radius. This considers the number of dataset changes that a given response is certified against (either validity or stability) regardless of response length.\nL225: First-Token Metrics. Modern jailbreaking attacks (cite75†Zou et al., 2023 ; cite76†Wei et al., 2023 ) succeed by manipulating the first token to bypass safety mechanisms (e.g., changing “Sorry, I cannot help” to “Sure, I can help”). We measure First Token Stability (FTS@k)—the fraction of test prompts where the first token remains unchanged under k-poisoning—and First Token Validity (FTV@k)—the fraction where the first token cannot be changed to the target harmful token $t_{1}$.\nL226: Formally, given a model $\\tilde{f}$ aligned on any dataset $\\widetilde{\\mathcal{D}}\\in\\mathcal{B}_{k}(\\mathcal{D})$ and test set $\\mathcal{D}_{t}$: $\\text{FTS@k}\\overset{\\Delta}{=}\\frac{1}{|\\mathcal{D}_{t}|}\\sum_{i=1}^{|\\mathcal{D}_{t}|}\\mathbb{I}\\left(f(x^{(i)})[0]=\\tilde{f}(x^{(i)})[0]\\right)$ with validity i.e., FTV@k simply replacing the inner indicator with the condition $\\tilde{f}(x^{(i)})[0]\\neq t_{i}$.\nL227: Horizon Metrics. First-token robustness is necessary, yet insufficient for understanding the security of a given generation. To fully characterize autoregressive generation, we must also understand how much of a given generation is secure. Thus, we measure Stability Horizon (SH@k) the average length of the longest certified stable prefix, and Validity Horizon (VH@k) the average length before harmful sequence $s_{h}=\\{t_{1},\\ldots,t_{T}\\}$ can be induced.\nL228: Formally these are defined as: $\\text{SH@k}\\overset{\\Delta}{=}\\frac{1}{|\\mathcal{D}_{t}|}\\sum_{i=1}^{|\\mathcal{D}_{t}|}\\max\\big\\{\\ell\\mid f(x^{(i)})[0:\\ell]=\\tilde{f}(x^{(i)})[0:\\ell]\\big\\}$ with VH@k changing the optimization term to $\\max\\big\\{\\ell\\mid\\tilde{f}(x^{(i)}+[t_{1}:t_{\\ell-1}])[0]\\neq t_{\\ell}\\big\\}$.\nL229: ### 5.2 Validity Certification for Agent Tool-Calling\nL230: \nL231: Autonomous agents increasingly rely on external tools through standardized interfaces like MCP. Ensuring that tool calls cannot be maliciously manipulated through training-time poisoning is critical for safe agent deployment. We evaluate TPA’s scalability and effectiveness by certifying validity of tool-calling under massive sharding ($S=500$), representing the first certified guarantees for agent systems.\nL232: We filter the Toucan dataset (cite77†Xu et al., 2025 ) by considering only the top 150 tools which leads to a dataset with 50,000 training instances that we partition into K=500 disjoint shards of 100 examples each on which we fine-tune OLMo-2-1B-Instruct (cite78†Groeneveld et al., 2024 ) using Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA, $r=8$) (cite79†Hu et al., 2022 ) and supervised fine-tuning.\nL233: We compare two training regimes: (1) Full LoRA, adapting all transformer layers, and (2) Last-3 LoRA, freezing the first 13 layers and training only the final 3. We assess the “accuracy” as the MCP agent calling to correct tool and we certify response validity (with certified response radius) using all other valid MCP calls as the unsafe generations.\nL234: Figure cite80†2 demonstrates TPA’s effectiveness for validity certification at scale. Panel (a) shows the distribution of certified robustness radii: Full LoRA achieves median $r{=}249$ (equal to the theoretical maximum $S/2{=}250$), meaning adversaries must poison at least 249 examples—0.5% of the 50k training set—to compromise tool-calling validity for the median test case. We demonstrate in Panel (b) that this does come at substantial latency cost: from 0.3s for zero-shot $\\to$ 150s.\nL235: We highlight that this is on a single L40 GPU where models are evaluated sequentially. Latency can be reduced back to 0.3s via parallelization. Outside of hardware acceleration, we investigate Last-3 LoRA which yields more dispersed radii (median $r{=}163$, corresponding to 0.33% poisoning resistance) but offers a $6\\times$ latency speed-up.\nL236: Panel (b) shows the accuracy-robustness tradeoff: Full LoRA achieves 67% test accuracy (significantly outperforming 40% zero-shot baseline), while Last-3 LoRA trades some accuracy (54%) for efficiency.\nL237: These results establish three important conclusions: (i) TPA scales to real-world agent systems—500 shards with hundreds of tools can be trained and deployed in under 2 hours on a single GPU; (ii) certified validity is strong—median resistance to 0.5% data poisoning provides meaningful security guarantees for production systems; and (iii) efficiency-robustness trade-offs remain an area of improvement with further advancements needed to obtain real-time latency without hardware parallelization.\nL238: For the latter, we explore the trade-offs through a set of ablations in Appendix cite31†E .\nL239: ### 5.3 Certification of Secure Alignment\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nVideoWorld 2: Learning Transferable Knowledge from Real-world Videos (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.10102v1)\nciteturn31703view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn31698view2\",\"lineno\":261}); Total lines: 527\nL209: First, we stop the gradient from the decoder without using the reconstructed video (row 3) to verify if this decoder introduces noise. Compared to row 2, this yields a $\\sim$20% improvement in success rate, suggesting that the original decoder indeed introduces extraneous noise that degrades latent representation performance. Second, we investigate the utility of the reconstructed video itself.\nL210: We find that utilizing this motion conditioning (rows 4 and 5) stabilizes video output, yielding a substantial improvement of $\\sim$0.9 in LPIPS and up to $\\sim$20% in task success rate. This benefit is more pronounced in the longer paper folding task compared to block stacking, demonstrating the effectiveness of our method for long-horizon video generation.\nL211: Pre-trained VDM  | Decoder Stop-Grad  | Ctrl- Net  | Video-CraftBench\nL212: Paper  | Block  | LPIPS$\\downarrow$\nL213:  |  |  | 0.0  | 28.5  | 0.312\nL214: ✓  |  |  | 30.3  | 45.2  | 0.297\nL215: ✓  | ✓  |  | 47.3  | 54.7  | 0.275\nL216: ✓  |  | ✓  | 51.1  | 52.0  | 0.213\nL217: ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | 68.8  | 77.5  | 0.205\nL218: (a)\nL219: Query length $N$  | Video-CraftBench  | CALVIN\nL220: Paper  | Block  | LPIPS$\\downarrow$  | Avg.Len.\nL221: baseline  | 0.0  | 28.5  | 0.312  | 1.11\nL222: 1  | 41.9  | 65.0  | 0.221  | 1.53\nL223: 2  | 55.1  | 69.5  | 0.210  | 1.64\nL224: 4  | 68.8  | 77.5  | 0.205  | 1.87\nL225: 8  | 65.0  | 77.5  | 0.195  | 1.88\nL226: (b)\nL227: \nL228: Proj  | Interplay  | Paper  | Block\nL229: Layer  | Attn type\nL230: MLP  | cross  | 52.0  | 61.3\nL231: +self  | cross  | 52.3  | 61.8\nL232: MLP  | causal cross  | 69.8  | 78.6\nL233: +self  | causal cross  | 72.3  | 80.9\nL234: (c)\nL235: Codebook size  | Video-CraftBench  | CALVIN\nL236: Paper  | Block  | LPIPS$\\downarrow$  | Avg.Len.\nL237: baseline  | 0.0  | 28.5  | 0.312  | 1.11\nL238: 8  | 20.1  | 29.9  | 0.258  | 1.65\nL239: 1000  | 68.8  | 77.5  | 0.205  | 1.87\nL240: 4096  | 50.4  | 59.6  | 0.208  | 1.90\nL241: 64,000  | 29.4  | 36.0  | 0.230  | 1.89\nL242: (d)\nL243: \nL244: Length $T$  | Video-CraftBench  | CALVIN\nL245: Paper  | Block  | Avg.Len.\nL246: 2  | 19.1  | 38.7  | 1.55\nL247: 9  | 55.4  | 68.7  | 1.61\nL248: 49  | 65.3  | 76.2  | 1.80\nL249: 93  | 68.8  | 77.5  | 1.87\nL250: 177  | 69.0  | 76.8  | 1.79\nL251: (e)\nL252: Training Strategy  | Video-CraftBench\nL253: Paper  | Block\nL254: baseline  | 0.0  | 28.5\nL255: random  | 0.0  | 0.0\nL256: freeze  | 31.7  | 40.2\nL257: lora  | 50.9  | 62.3\nL258: full  | 68.8  | 77.5\nL259: (f)\nL260: Table 3: Ablation studies. We train with Video-CraftBench only and evaluate CALVIN in its in-domain setting. cite118†Image: Refer to caption Figure 7: UMAP of latent codes. With a pretrained VDM, latent codes for the same action align more tightly than those in VideoWorld [cite22†51 ] across environments, indicating more consistent and transferable dynamics.\nL261: (Left) In distinct environments like Bridge [cite117†58 ] and CALVIN [cite38†45 ], latent representations of the same action (e.g., the robotic arm moving right) are highly similar. (Right) Conversely, in VideoWorld, latent codes for the same action exhibit significant divergence across environments and fail to cluster effectively in the UMAP visualization. cite119†Image: Refer to caption Figure 8: Visualization of failure cases in other baselines on the Video-CraftBench. Best viewed in color.\nL262: Query embedding length of dLDM. Tab. LABEL:tab:abla_N ablates the number of dLDM query embeddings $N$. A larger $N$ captures more information but also risks encoding noise and increases the sequence length for the AR transformer. We find that $N=1$ already achieves a respectable task success rate, while $N=4$ provides the best performance balance. Increasing $N$ to 8 offers a slight LPIPS benefit but introduces more noise, which reduces the overall success rate.\nL263: Interplay between VDM and LDM. Tab. LABEL:tab:abla_interplay ablates the interplay mechanism between the LDM and VDM, which consists of a projection layer (MLP and causal self-attention) and causal cross-attention. The causal cross-attention ensures generation relies solely on the current time step’s latents, effectively preventing information leakage.\nL264: Results show that incorporating self-attention into the projection layer and utilizing causal cross-attention significantly enhance LDM-VDM interaction, validating the effectiveness of our design\nL265: Horizon length in dLDM. Tab. LABEL:tab:abla_T ablates the dLDM context length $T$. At $T=2$ (a LAPA-like setting), codes remain transferable, but performance on the long-range paper folding task is poor due to a lack of temporal perception. This minimal context also provides low-quality motion guidance for the VDM, leading to poor LPIPS scores. Performance improves as $T$ increases, plateauing at $T=93$, which corresponds to the maximum context length of our Cosmos VDM.\nL266: dLDM codebook size. Tab. LABEL:tab:abla_codebooksize examines the impact of the dLDM codebook size (adjusted via FSQ levels) on policy learning. We find that CALVIN, with its simpler action space, benefits from a relatively small codebook. In contrast, the more complex Video-CraftBench requires a larger codebook to achieve significant improvements.\nL267: However, an excessively large codebook risks encoding extraneous noise and can hinder dLDM training convergence, a finding consistent with VideoWorld [cite22†51 ].\nL268: Training strategies of VDM. In Tab. LABEL:tab:abla_vdm, we examine different training configurations for the video diffusion model. Our default approach fully fine-tunes the pre-trained VDM to exploit its appearance priors. In contrast, training a randomly initialized VDM (row 1) leads to model collapse and fails to generate valid videos. We also experiment with freezing the pre-trained VDM and updating only the VQ-VAE components (encoder, quantizer, and projection layer).\nL269: Although this setup yields performance gains, it falls significantly short of LoRA or full fine-tuning. We believe this gap exists because the VDM needs further adaptation to capture the fine-grained manipulation details specific to Video-CraftBench.\nL270: ## 6 Further Discussion with Other Works\nL271: \nL272: We provide a comprehensive analysis, highlighting the differences between VideoWorld 2 and prior works in both objectives and methodology.\nL273: Regard task objectives. Most prior works focus on short-horizon prediction, simulated control, or reconstruction-centric tasks. In contrast, VideoWorld 2 targets complex, minute-long real-world tasks, where appearance variation and long-horizon error accumulation are dominant. This exposes appearance interference as a critical bottleneck, necessitating our dLDM.\nL274: Regarding “disentanglement”. Prior works [cite120†63 , cite121†66 , cite114†67 , cite94†13 , cite22†51 , cite88†72 ] rely on VAE-style reconstruction objectives to separate dynamics from appearance. As demonstrated in VideoWorld 2, this approach is insufficient for complex real-world videos, as reconstruction objectives compel latent codes to encode task-irrelevant details.\nL275: VideoWorld 2 instead mitigates appearance interference by leveraging a pretrained VDM for appearance modeling, forcing latents to focus on task-relevant dynamics. While CoLA [cite113†62 ] also employs a VDM, it is limited to short 2-frame transitions. In contrast, VideoWorld 2 models multi-step dynamics and reuses coarse VAE decoder outputs to provide structured temporal cues—a mechanism critical for long-horizon tasks (Tab. LABEL:tab:abla_arch, rows 3 vs. 5).\nL276: Replacing our dLDM with prior works’ tokenizers [cite114†67 ] or adopting CoLA’s training scheme significantly degrades long-sequence performance, validating our design.\nL277: Regarding the effect of the VDM. The role of the VDM in VideoWorld 2 also differs from prior approaches. Works such as $\\epsilon$-VAE [cite122†80 ] and DiVoT [cite123†26 ] utilize VDMs for pixel-level reconstruction, necessitating that latent codes encode both appearance and dynamics. Others, like IGOR [cite94†13 ] and AdaWorld [cite91†24 ], employ VDMs primarily for rendering. This disconnects the VDM from latent optimization, failing to improve task objectives.\nL278: In contrast, VideoWorld 2 offloads appearance modeling to the VDM, compelling latents to focus exclusively on task-relevant dynamics. Compared to CoLA [cite113†62 ], VideoWorld 2 benefits from multi-frame modeling and a VDM architecture specifically tailored for long-horizon learning.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSample-Efficient Real-World Dexterous Policy Fine-Tuning via Action-Chunked Critics and Normalizing Flows (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.09580v1)\nciteturn31703view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn31698view1\",\"lineno\":175}); Total lines: 710\nL158:  | $\\begin{aligned} \\theta=\\arg\\max_{\\theta}\\mathbb{E}_{s_{t}\\sim\\mathcal{D}}\\mathbb{E}_{a\\sim\\pi_{\\theta}}\\left[Q_{\\bar{\\phi}}(s_{t},a)\\right],\\text{s.t. }D(\\pi_{\\theta},\\pi_{\\beta})<\\epsilon,\\end{aligned}$  |  | (4)\nL159: \nL160: where $D(\\cdot,\\cdot)$ is a divergence measure and $\\epsilon$ controls the degree of regularization.\nL161: ##### Conditional normalizing flows\nL162: \nL163: Normalizing flows (NFs) [cite105†42 ] are expressive models capable of representing multimodal policies over high-dimensional action chunks [cite92†49 , cite91†29 ] and can be simply trained with reinforcement learning [cite90†18 ]. An input latent variable $z_{0}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}$ is sampled from a base distribution $p_{0}$, and transformed through a sequence of invertible mappings $f_{k}(\\cdot;c)$ conditioned on $c\\in\\mathbb{R}^{m}$:\nL164:  | $$z_{K}=f_{\\theta}(z_{0};c)=f_{K}(\\cdots f_{2}(f_{1}(z_{0};c);c);c),$$  |  | (5)\nL165: \nL166: where $c$ typically encodes the current observation. The conditional density follows from the change-of-variables formula:\nL167: \nL168:  | $$\\log p_{\\theta}(z_{K}\\mid c)=\\log p_{0}(z_{0})-\\sum_{k=1}^{K}\\log\\left|\\det\\frac{\\partial f_{k}(\\cdot;c)}{\\partial z_{k-1}}\\right|.$$  |  | (6)\nL169: Maximizing (cite106†6 ) enables exact likelihood evaluation and efficient sampling. As a policy parameterization, this allows us to sample action chunks $\\mathbf{a}_{k}\\sim\\pi_{\\theta}(\\cdot\\mid o_{k})$ and compute $\\log\\pi_{\\theta}(\\mathbf{a}_{k}\\mid o_{k})$, which is critical for conservative regularization toward the behavior policy $\\pi_{\\beta}$ during offline and online fine-tuning.\nL170: ## IV Method\nL171: cite107†Image: Refer to caption Fig. 1: Actor–critic architecture of SOFT-FLOW. The actor is a conditional normalizing flow (NF) that models an invertible mapping between action chunks and a base Gaussian distribution. It consists of $K$ stacked NF blocks, each applying affine transformations to a subset of tokens conditioning on observations. In the forward process, sampled actions are mapped to the base distribution, yielding tractable log-likelihoods used for behavior cloning supervision (cite108†8 ).\nL172: In the reverse direction, latent samples drawn from the Gaussian are transformed into actions through fully differentiable operations, enabling gradient-based policy optimization using the critic. The critic is a transformer-based Q-network that predicts action-chunk values conditioned on observations. Q-values are parameterized using an HL-Gaussian distribution to improve regression stability.\nL173: To mitigate overestimation bias, the final Q estimate is computed by taking the minimum over multiple critic predictions.\nL174: ### IV-A Setting\nL175: We learn a visuomotor policy $\\pi_{\\theta}(\\mathbf{a}\\mid o)$ and deploy it in a real robotic environment that provides observations $o_{t}$ and rewards $r_{t}$. The robot executes action chunks of horizon $H$: at each decision step $k$, the policy samples a sequence $\\mathbf{a}_{k}=(a_{k,0},\\dots,a_{k,H-1})$, which is executed open-loop (or with low-level stabilization).\nL176: Observations $o_{t}$ include camera images, robot proprioception, and a small number of actions from the previous chunk that have not yet been executed. This enables real-time chunking (RTC) [cite109†7 ] and provides the policy with context about its recent action history. We found RTC to be helpful not only because of improved inference time but also in terms of improving the performance thanks to the context about previous actions.\nL177: ### IV-B Normalizing-flow policy\nL178: \nL179: We parameterize the policy $\\pi_{\\theta}(\\mathbf{a}\\mid o)$ as a conditional normalizing flow, which enables efficient sampling, end-to-end differentiation and exact log-likelihood evaluation. This property is critical for seamless integration into RL pipeline and conservative regularization toward an initial policy during offline and online fine-tuning.\nL180: We implement each normalizing-flow transformation $f_{k}(\\cdot;c)$ using a transformer-based architecture [cite110†26 ]. Inspired by NinA [cite92†49 ], we adopt coupling layers similar to RealNVP [cite111†13 ]. During the forward pass, ground-truth actions (normalized to $[-1,1]$ using dataset statistics) are first perturbed with small Gaussian noise $\\mathcal{N}(0,\\sigma^{2}_{\\text{noise}})$, which has been shown to be crucial for stable action modeling [cite90†18 , cite92†49 ].\nL181: To enable bounded action space support, we then apply an element-wise $\\tanh^{-1}$ transformation as the next step.\nL182: Each flow step operates on latent variables $z_{k}=[\\mathbf{z}_{1},\\dots,\\mathbf{z}_{H}]$, where each element corresponds to one action in the chunk. The variables are randomly partitioned into two equally sized subsets $x_{k_{1}}$ and $x_{k_{2}}$. Conditioned on $c$, $x_{k_{1}}$ is processed by a nonlinear transformation $g_{k}(x_{k_{1}},c)$ implemented via stacked self-attention over $x_{k_{1}}$ and cross-attention between $x_{k_{1}}$ and $c$.\nL183: The outputs $s$ and $b$ parameterize an affine transformation applied to $x_{k_{2}}$:\nL184:  | $$y_{2}=\\exp(\\tanh(s))\\odot x_{k_{2}}+b,$$  |\nL185: while $y_{1}=x_{k_{1}}$. Concatenating $y_{1}$ and $y_{2}$ produces $z_{k-1}$. This design ensures invertibility and enables efficient computation of the Jacobian determinant. The final latent variable $z_{0}$ is used to compute $\\log p_{0}(z)$, allowing optimization of the exact log-likelihood in (cite106†6 ). The inverse pass applies the same transformations in reverse order. A schematic overview is shown in Fig. cite112†1 , and pseudocode is provided in Alg. cite113†1 .\nL186: When using $\\mathcal{N}(0,I)$ as $p_{0}(z)$ for training we can sample from $\\mathcal{N}(0,\\sigma_{sample}^{2}),\\sigma_{sample}\\leq 1$ during inference to obtain more likely actions which can help to improve performance [cite91†29 ].\nL187: ### IV-C Action-chunked critic\nL188: \nL189: Following cite97†Li et al. [28] , we learn a critic that matches the chunked control interface:\nL190: \nL191:  | $$Q_{\\phi}(o_{k},\\mathbf{a}_{k})\\approx\\mathbb{E}\\Big[\\sum_{i=0}^{H-1}\\gamma^{i}r_{k,i}+\\gamma^{H}V_{\\phi}(o_{k+1})\\Big],$$  |  | (7)\nL192: \nL193: where $o_{k+1}$ denotes the observation at the next decision boundary.\nL194: Inspired by recent findings on value learning stability [cite114†15 , cite115†46 ], we parameterize the critic as a categorical distribution trained using a cross-entropy objective. We employ HL-Gauss [cite116†22 ] as the distributional parameterization, which improves stability and scalability in practice.\nL195: \nL196: Algorithm 1 Normalizing-flow policy over action chunks\nL197: \nL198: Input: Observation $o$, base distribution $p_{0}(z)$, invertible flow $f_{\\theta}(\\cdot;\\,o)$\nL199: \nL200: $c\\leftarrow\\mathrm{Enc}(o)$\nL201: Forward pass (likelihood evaluation):\nL202: \nL203: $z_{0}\\leftarrow f_{\\theta}(a;\\,c)$ $\\triangleright$ actions $\\rightarrow$ latent\nL204: \nL205: $\\log\\pi_{\\theta}(a\\mid o)\\leftarrow\\log p_{0}(z_{0})+\\log\\left|\\det J_{f_{\\theta}}(a;\\,c)\\right|$\nL206: \nL207: Inverse pass (action generation):\nL208: \nL209: $z_{0}\\sim p_{0}(z)$\nL210: \nL211: $a\\leftarrow f_{\\theta}^{-1}(z_{0};\\,c)$ $\\triangleright$ latent $\\rightarrow$ actions\nL212: \nL213: $\\log\\pi_{\\theta}(a\\mid o)\\leftarrow\\log p_{0}(z_{0})-\\log\\left|\\det J_{f_{\\theta}^{-1}}(z_{0};\\,c)\\right|$\nL214: Algorithm 2 Action-chunk selection with critic evaluation\nL215: \nL216: Input: Observation $o_{k}$, policy $\\pi_{\\theta}$, critic $Q_{\\phi}$, number of samples $N_{\\pi}$\nL217: \nL218: Output: Selected action chunk $a_{k}$\nL219: \nL220: $\\triangleright$ Sample and score candidate chunks\nL221: \nL222: for $i=1$ to $N_{\\pi}$ do\nL223: \nL224:    $a^{(i)}\\sim\\pi_{\\theta}(\\cdot\\mid o_{k})$\nL225: \nL226:    $q^{(i)}\\leftarrow\\min_{j}Q_{\\phi}^{(j)}(o_{k},a^{(i)})$\nL227: \nL228: $a_{k}\\leftarrow\\arg\\max_{i}q^{(i)}$\nL229: \nL230: return $a_{k}$\nL231: \nL232: Algorithm 3 SOFT-FLOW training pipeline\nL233: Input: Demo data $\\mathcal{D}_{\\text{demo}}$, offline data $\\mathcal{D}$\nL234: \nL235: Output: Fine-tuned policy $\\pi_{\\theta}$ and critic $Q_{\\phi}$\nL236: \nL237: $\\triangleright$ Stage I: Imitation learning\nL238: \nL239: foreach minibatch $(o,a)\\sim\\mathcal{D}_{\\text{demo}}$ do\nL240: \nL241:    $\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{IL}}\\leftarrow-\\mathbb{E}[\\log\\pi_{\\theta}(a\\mid o)]$\nL242: \nL243:    $\\theta\\leftarrow\\theta-\\eta_{\\pi}\\nabla_{\\theta}\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{IL}}$\nL244: \nL245: $\\triangleright$ Stage II: Offline critic warm-up\nL246: \nL247: repeat\nL248:    $\\hat{a}_{k+1}\\sim\\pi_{\\theta_{0}}(\\cdot\\mid o_{k+1})$\nL249: \nL250:    $y\\leftarrow\\sum_{t=0}^{H-1}\\gamma^{t}r_{k,t}+\\gamma^{H}(1-d)Q_{\\bar{\\phi}}(o_{k+1},\\hat{a}_{k+1})$\nL251: \nL252:    $\\mathcal{L}_{Q}\\leftarrow\\text{CrossEntropy}(Q_{\\phi}(o_{k},a_{k}),y)$\nL253: \nL254:    $\\phi\\leftarrow\\phi-\\eta_{Q}\\nabla_{\\phi}\\mathcal{L}_{Q}$\nL255: \nL256: until convergence on $\\mathcal{D}$\nL257: \nL258: $\\triangleright$ Stage III: Full offline RL\nL259: \nL260: repeat\nL261: \nL262:    Update critic as above\nL263: \nL264:    $a_{\\pi_{\\theta}}\\leftarrow$ Alg. cite117†2 "
